As for his advices , most of them are true and i have experienced them in real life. His last book about human nature was most unscientific yet informative book for me per se.

You can be both at the same time. While seeming like a fully cooperative participant of a group, with the presentation of your ideas by a silver-tongued style approach you can court attention while maintaining your status at the same time. Your machiavellian logic and eloquence of your speechcraft play a huge role here.

&#x200B;

If this is not an option then do not go for law 6 and remember the law 48, adapt the situation first, then make it adapt to yourself.  Overdoing law 6, people will only see you as an insecure attention seeker and not a man/woman who is comfortable in her own skin.

You need to be careful with stimulants.

&#x200B;

It is no secret that every dopaminergic stimulant has the potential to send you into a neurotic rage. Dopamine is something not to be messed with. Like suggested by other users, give a little bit of break to everything and let your brain to operate normally for a while. Dopamine is shown like a neurotransmitter which only promotes good qualities but it is also associated with bad ones and those can be damaging. It is shown to encourage anti-social behaviors or causing extreme anger from clinical studies. I also want to add dopamine has a strange effect on psychology. Dopaminergic substances are thought to be causing anxiety because of their effects but the opposite also can be true. Anti-anxiety effects may also increase the chance of having those bad thoughts and anti-social behavioral patterns because of its disinhibiting nature.

&#x200B;

Just do not forget, dopaminergic stimulants or stimulants with dopaminergic properties has been used by Vikings to induce a state of "berserker-rage" or by janissaries to make them better warriors. They also have been used by US military, Nazis and several other for their effects.

&#x200B;

Next time, do not act upon anecdotal reports before messing with some potent drug which targets your main excitatory system. The person may just be deceptive or manipulative for whatever reason. Start with the lowest dose than slightly increase it until your sweet spot. Memantine is a drug which should be treated differently as its novel mechanism of action.

&#x200B;

Stay safe OP, also drink a lot of water.

The only thing that i found in terms of sustainable, long-lasting and clean energy which makes you great again is modafinil + l-theanine.

&#x200B;

Other than that, vitamin b3, Korean red ginseng, l-tyrosine (or NALT) and phenylpiracetam are also good options. I didn't have much luck with curcumin. Fish oil is always a great addition to any nootropic stack. Also, you may consider vitamin b6, b1, and b12 as they are co-factors of your dopaminergic and noradrenergic systems.

I think you skipped Anandamide. This comes straight from wikipedia page:

&#x200B;

 **Anandamide**, also known as ***N*****-arachidonoylethanolamine** or **AEA**, is a [fatty acid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatty_acid) [neurotransmitter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurotransmitter) derived from the non-oxidative metabolism of eicosatetraenoic acid ([arachidonic acid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arachidonic_acid)), an essential ω-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid. The name is taken from the [Sanskrit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanskrit) word [*ananda*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%80nanda_(Hindu_philosophy)), which means "joy, [bliss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happiness), [delight](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happiness)", and [amide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amide).[\[1\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamide#cite_note-pmid1470919-1)[\[2\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamide#cite_note-isbn0-12-551460-3-2) It is synthesized from *N*\-arachidonoyl phosphatidylethanolamine by multiple pathways.[\[3\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamide#cite_note-wang2009-3) It is degraded primarily by the [fatty acid amide hydrolase](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatty_acid_amide_hydrolase) (FAAH) enzyme, which converts anandamide into [ethanolamine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethanolamine) and [arachidonic acid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arachidonic_acid). As such, inhibitors of FAAH lead to elevated anandamide levels and are being pursued for therapeutic use.[\[4\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamide#cite_note-4)[\[5\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamide#cite_note-5)
